p.src:
Stationarity requires Eq.  to vanish for every collection of functions $f_i(t)$. The arbitrary-function proposition applies separately to every $i$, giving $\boxed{
 \frac{d}{dt}
 \left(\frac{\partial L}{\partial\dot q_i}\right)
 =
 \frac{\partial L}{\partial q_i}
 }
 \qquad (i=1,\ldots,n).$ These are the Euler–Lagrange equations. The global condition $\delta A=0$ has become one local differential equation for each generalized coordinate.

