Set $d=x_1-x_2$. The chain rule gives $\begin{aligned}
 \frac{\partial U}{\partial x_1}
 &=\frac{dU}{dd},
 &
 \frac{\partial U}{\partial x_2}
 &=-\frac{dU}{dd}.
 \end{aligned}$ Therefore $\begin{aligned}
 \dot p_1&=-\frac{\partial U}{\partial x_1},
 &
 \dot p_2&=-\frac{\partial U}{\partial x_2}
          =+\frac{\partial U}{\partial x_1}.\end{aligned}$ The forces are equal and opposite, and adding the equations gives $\frac{d}{dt}(p_1+p_2)=0.$

